Sval: Settings' Theme sets the window's light or dark scheme

Firefox's Website appearance control writes
layout.css.prefers-color-scheme.content-override, which
PreferenceSheet::Prefs::Load reads for content documents alone. A chrome
document's scheme comes from the theme: LightweightThemeConsumer sets the
window's BrowsingContext.prefersColorSchemeOverride and
browser.theme.toolbar-theme, and for the default theme it picks dark from
(-moz-system-dark-theme) only. Sval keeps the default theme (decision 0093), so
the window stayed with the system's scheme whatever was chosen.

LightweightThemeConsumer is the one writer of the window's scheme, so the
choice is read there: Dark makes the default theme's dark variant apply, Light
makes it light, Automatic keeps the system's. The control is renamed Theme,
with text saying it sets Sval and what websites are told. No chrome API reaches
the consumer's decision; a second writer of the window's override would race it.

